WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.835 --> 00:00:03.552
A new program is ready for the machining centre.

2
00:00:04.289 --> 00:00:07.505
The setter reads it first, before the machine sees it.

3
00:00:08.598 --> 00:00:10.514
A program is a list of lines.

4
00:00:11.228 --> 00:00:12.853
Each line is called a block.

5
00:00:13.613 --> 00:00:17.421
The control reads them from top to bottom, one block at a time.

6
00:00:18.439 --> 00:00:22.189
A block starts with a line number, such as N10 or N20.

7
00:00:23.026 --> 00:00:24.733
Then follow a few words.

8
00:00:25.447 --> 00:00:27.351
A word is a letter plus a number.

9
00:00:28.376 --> 00:00:30.129
G words say how to move.

10
00:00:30.882 --> 00:00:33.100
X, Y and Z say where to.

11
00:00:34.152 --> 00:00:37.344
F is the feed, on a mill in millimetres per minute.

12
00:00:38.119 --> 00:00:41.219
S is the spindle speed in revolutions per minute.

13
00:00:41.965 --> 00:00:43.347
T is the tool number.

14
00:00:44.684 --> 00:00:46.112
Most codes are modal.

15
00:00:46.809 --> 00:00:50.884
They stay active until another code of the same group replaces them.

16
00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:53.633
Write G01 once,

17
00:00:53.819 --> 00:00:57.894
and every following line with only coordinates is also a straight cut.

18
00:00:58.664 --> 00:00:59.779
At the same F.

19
00:01:00.797 --> 00:01:02.562
That keeps programs short.

20
00:01:03.297 --> 00:01:06.188
But you must read upwards to know what a line does.

21
00:01:07.217 --> 00:01:10.096
G90 switches on absolute programming.

22
00:01:10.816 --> 00:01:15.680
X20 then means 20 mm from the zero point, G54.

23
00:01:16.678 --> 00:01:19.987
G91 switches on incremental programming.

24
00:01:20.706 --> 00:01:25.060
X20 then means 20 mm further than where the tool is now.

25
00:01:25.823 --> 00:01:28.017
Mixing them up is a classic crash.

26
00:01:29.348 --> 00:01:30.822
Now the motion codes.

27
00:01:31.591 --> 00:01:37.233
G00 is a rapid move, as fast as the machine can go and never in the material.

28
00:01:38.207 --> 00:01:41.214
G01 is a straight cut at feed F.

29
00:01:41.926 --> 00:01:46.617
G02 is an arc clockwise and G03 anticlockwise.

30
00:01:47.341 --> 00:01:48.595
R is the radius.

31
00:01:49.585 --> 00:01:51.698
An M-code is a machine switch.

32
00:01:52.431 --> 00:01:55.578
M03 turns the spindle on, clockwise.

33
00:01:56.340 --> 00:02:00.729
M05 is spindle stop and M06 is tool change.

34
00:02:01.758 --> 00:02:06.588
M08 switches the coolant on and M09 switches it off.

35
00:02:07.286 --> 00:02:09.457
M30 is the end of the program.

36
00:02:10.463 --> 00:02:12.286
Every control has extras.

37
00:02:13.004 --> 00:02:18.449
But these basics follow ISO 6983 and are almost the same everywhere.

38
00:02:19.799 --> 00:02:22.179
This is how a setter reads a new program.

39
00:02:22.931 --> 00:02:26.716
First the top, with the work offset, the tools and the speeds.

40
00:02:27.749 --> 00:02:29.688
Then the first moves of each tool.

41
00:02:30.381 --> 00:02:32.169
Always with the same question.

42
00:02:32.889 --> 00:02:35.270
Where is the tool now, and where will it go?

43
00:02:36.259 --> 00:02:39.243
The dangerous lines are rapid moves near the part.

44
00:02:39.992 --> 00:02:44.451
A G00 to Z2, 2 mm above the part, is fine.

45
00:02:45.152 --> 00:02:50.040
A G00 to Z minus 3 goes into the material at full speed.

46
00:02:51.119 --> 00:02:54.916
That is why the setter watches the distance-to-go during the first run.

47
00:02:55.635 --> 00:02:58.526
That is how far the current move still has to go.

48
00:02:59.872 --> 00:03:03.889
The example is a slot in a steel plate, 3 mm deep.

49
00:03:04.655 --> 00:03:07.929
It is cut with a 10 mm end mill, tool 3.

50
00:03:08.644 --> 00:03:09.747
This is the program.

51
00:03:10.773 --> 00:03:14.372
N10 is absolute, from zero point G54.

52
00:03:15.102 --> 00:03:17.029
N20 puts tool 3 in.

53
00:03:18.047 --> 00:03:22.830
N30 gives 3,200 revolutions per minute, clockwise.

54
00:03:23.579 --> 00:03:27.596
N40 goes in rapid to 25 mm above the part.

55
00:03:28.354 --> 00:03:32.371
The tool length is switched on, but the next level explains that code.

56
00:03:33.130 --> 00:03:34.314
The coolant goes on.

57
00:03:35.350 --> 00:03:37.637
N50 is a rapid above the start.

58
00:03:38.413 --> 00:03:44.381
N60 feeds down to 3 mm deep, at 150 mm/min.

59
00:03:45.449 --> 00:03:52.474
N70 is still G01, a straight cut of 50 mm, from Y minus 10 to Y40.

60
00:03:53.249 --> 00:03:56.035
At 640 mm per minute.

61
00:03:56.837 --> 00:04:01.922
That comes from 0.05 mm per tooth, times 4 teeth,

62
00:04:02.165 --> 00:04:05.648
times 3,200 revolutions per minute.

63
00:04:06.439 --> 00:04:07.530
That is an assumption.

64
00:04:08.551 --> 00:04:13.125
N80 is a quarter circle of radius 20 mm, clockwise.

65
00:04:13.847 --> 00:04:16.761
N90 goes up and switches the coolant off.

66
00:04:17.491 --> 00:04:19.302
N100 is the end.

67
00:04:20.645 --> 00:04:21.910
One thing to fix.

68
00:04:22.661 --> 00:04:30.730
N60 feeds 28 mm, from Z25 to Z minus 3, at 150 mm per minute.

69
00:04:31.509 --> 00:04:34.493
That takes 11 seconds, mostly through air.

70
00:04:35.516 --> 00:04:40.334
A G00 to Z2 first leaves only 5 mm at feed.

71
00:04:41.107 --> 00:04:42.372
That is 2 seconds.

72
00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:49.193
On 2,000 parts that saves 9 times 2,000 which is 18,000 seconds.

73
00:04:49.909 --> 00:04:51.209
That is 5 hours.
